        connectiveDiscriminant gloss="alternation emitted as conjunction" symptom="A generated parse condition joins a rule's options with AND where any one of them should do, and a census of the emitted bodies finds not a single OR anywhere in the population." finding="setParse hangs builtinParsE and builtinActoR -- both noPrint decoration -- on every rule it touches, so hasAttributeS, which answers is-this-node-marked-up, reads TRUE for every walked rule and the OR branch is unreachable. MEASURED 2026-08-26 as a 2x2 over parser(Start), 55 bodies: with setParse running 36 AND / 0 OR, with setParse off 28 AND / 8 OR, gate spelling irrelevant in both columns. A SECOND MECHANISM SAT UNDER IT: setParse adds each decoration attribute BEFORE marking it noPrint, so addAttribute -- the flag writer -- cannot see that decoration is decoration at the one instant it looks, and a replacement flag asking the right question still answered wrongly. Both are fixed; the live row now reads 28 AND / 8 OR in both columns." workaround="None. Nothing is built on the connective being right yet, because no generated parse has been trusted for a whole-grammar answer." solution="FIXED IN TWO RULED PARTS. 2026-08-26: hasTraits, a GroupBody flag raised only by an attribute that is NOT noPrint-class, at four registration sites with addAttribute and updateContentFlags as writers, and generateParse's gate swapped to it. 2026-08-27: setParse calls field.updateContentFlags() once after both decoration attributes are fully formed -- a rebuild at exit rather than a reorder. A third edit was forced and unplanned: updateContentFlags read listLength through an unguarded groupList, latent while moveTo was its only caller, fatal the moment setParse handed it leaves. STEP-AND-BLESSED by Tony 2026-08-27; the citizen promoted out of the queue into the fleet as incant/connectiveT." status="remedy" verdict="-- awaiting Tony --" reviewed="-- unreviewed --" station="not a frontier station; parser iteration 2 docket, promoted by ruling 2026-08-26, discharged by remedy 2026-08-27" vintage="2026-08-27" site="seven sites: GroupBody.twk and groups.ext for the bit, incant/setup GroupFields, Instruct.rtn opDot case 42, GroupItem.twk addAttribute and updateContentFlags, Generate.rtn setParse, and IncantForms/WorkingOn/parser generateParse for the gate; fleet fixture incant/connectiveT, promoted 2026-08-27 from incant/fixits/connectiveDiscriminant, which is its VINTAGE name -- it promoted to the fleet as incant/connectiveT on 2026-08-27" beartraps="bt26 bt28 firstCallerNullList parseSelfRecursion" description="⚠ IT IS A CORRECTNESS THREAT AND NOT A CRASH CANDIDATE, and the distinction is the reason it got its own row rather than a line on the recursion docket. An alternation emitted as a conjunction produces a WRONG parse, never an unbounded one. So it survives every instrument the recursion work is using: a future parser(Start) that runs to completion could still parse incorrectly, with the verifier's printed ANSWER the only guard between a wrong parse and a green run.
