        parseSelfRecursion gloss="generated parse re-enters itself" symptom="A generated-parse walk completes and prints its sentinel, and then the verification call dies at SIGSEGV with tens of thousands of identical stack frames naming one action." finding="CONFIRMED BY PRINT 2026-08-27, in pointers. aCTionStatemenT dispatches statement->groupBody->gMethod(statement) at GroupRules.mm:1487; under a walked-and-compiled grammar that gMethod resolves to aCTionStatemenT itself, and the node handed down is THE SAME NODE every time -- input == statement true, and input identical at 0x...5f140 across 35 consecutive frames read at depths 6, 8, 20 and 30, tag StatemenT throughout. Period one, in pointers rather than by frame-shape inference. ⚠ AND getGroup IS NOT INVOLVED AT ALL: StatemenT reads datA 0, not isGROUP, so the guard above it is false and the call never fires. statement is the plain assignment from input and nothing between touches it. 74705 frames, guard page touched by the allocation at GroupRules.mm:1475." workaround="Turn either gate off. The crash needs BOTH compiling and activating on; any other cell of the 2x2 completes at exit 0 and reaches the verify sentinel." solution="CANDIDATE 2 PROMOTED TO RULED, 2026-08-27, on the confirming print. The mechanism is settled: one node, its own action, dispatched at GroupRules.mm:1487. ⚠ THE FIX SHAPE IS NOT SELECTED -- the ruling is about the DIAGNOSIS, and the fix conversation opens on that dispatch line. Nothing is buildable from this row yet." status="ruled" verdict="-- awaiting Tony --" reviewed="-- unreviewed --" station="not a frontier station; parser iteration 2, 2026-08-26; confirming print 2026-08-27" vintage="2026-08-27" site="GroupRules.mm:1487 is the recursing dispatch; GroupRules.mm:1475 is the allocation that touches the guard page; IncantForms/WorkingOn/parser is the caller, with BOTH gates on. ⚠ ERRATUM: the 2026-08-26 docket cited :1483 for getGroup. At that same vintage getGroup is at :1484 and :1483 is the isGROUP guard above it -- bear-trap 36 exactly, a line number naming the test rather than the call. The call is moot in any case; it never fires." beartraps="bt34 bt26" description="⚠⚠ THE DOOR, 2026-08-27 SECOND PRINT -- AND THE ENTRY PATH IS NOT WHAT THE runAction RECONSTRUCTION SAID. Read outermost-inward from the stack bottom, the frames below the first mirror entry are: runOP, runAction argument=StatemenT field=parser, processAction, aCTionBlocK, runOP, runOP, runRule field=ruleText rule=StatemenT, parseRule field=StatemenT, aCTionBlocK, then aCTionStatemenT. THREE CORRECTIONS. One, the runOP/runAction/processAction frames are the VERIFIER ACTION parser executing its own ordinary body, not a generated parse body -- runAction is running the action named parser with argument StatemenT, and the aCTionBlocK under it is parser's own block. Two, the rule-name call DID take the legal road: runRule then parseRule, which is the parse. Three, the first mirror entry is called by aCTionBlocK, not by runAction, and runAction contains no gMethod dispatch at all -- it calls processAction.
