            interpretBC=(THE C++ DISPATCH LOOP OVER A bcLIST, and it replaced an incant one.
                A plain C++ cursor sidesteps two entangled incant blockers that made the
                branch inexpressible in interpreted incant: grup := result WELDS the test
                variable to the branch-target node, and aCTionFOR advances its OWN C++
                cursor so a body := cannot steer the iteration. nextGroup is stateless, so
                a plain cursor relocates to an arbitrary branch-target member cleanly.
                The reasoning trail is docs/branch-mechanism.md; the resolution is
                docs/branch-dispatch-findings.md.

                runByteFn returns the branch-target stream member on a taken branch and
                null on every non-branch op, which is why the loop relocates by tag on a
                non-null and falls to nextMember otherwise.

                opStack is hung off the bcLIST here, at entry, so the bcOP handlers can
                reach it through opStackOf, which is parent.getAttribute of opStack. The
                file header states the same contract from the handlers' side.

                MOVED HERE FROM GroupActions.rtn 2026-09-04, cleanup arc. The method is
                unchanged; only its home and its comment moved.#);
            runByteFn=(TRACK A DISPATCH PRIMITIVE. A bytecode op carries a method-bound
                interpret child, built by interpretMethod. Invoking that child's method
                from incant is the poochifier -- = drops the binding, bear-trap 2 -- so
                interpretBC delegates here instead: fetch the child and call its bound
                handler in place, with the op as the instruction. No copy, no =.

                Label ops have no interpret child, so the lookup gives null and interpretBC
                treats the null return as a no-op fall-through to the next instruction.
                That is why the null case is a return rather than a refusal.

